{"turn":1,"elapsedMs":36681,"answer":"- **General chapter 13: “Oxytocin, OXTR and IL-6” — 42 sources.** [S1]\n- **General chapter 14: “IL-6 and mental health” — 120 peer-reviewed publications:** 117 directly relevant mental-health papers and three contextual exposure papers. [S2]\n\n**Chapter 13 is pathway-focused:** how oxytocin and its receptor, OXTR, affect IL-6 and other inflammation markers in cells, animals and humans. Selected laboratory models show strong effects, alongside null and opposite-direction results. Human findings are promising but mixed; sustained anti-inflammatory benefit is not established. [S1]\n\n**Chapter 14 is condition-focused:** IL-6 associations and intervention evidence across depression, schizophrenia/psychosis, bipolar depression, mania, hypomania, anxiety, PTSD, suicidality, ADHD and substance use disorders. It distinguishes association, biomarker change and clinical benefit, retaining diagnosis-specific gaps. IL-6 is not established as a universal cause of mental illness. [S2]\n\nThese are separate, complementary chapters. Linking OXTR interventions to mental-health improvement through IL-6 remains a hypothesis. Source counts are not independent-study counts. [S1][S2]","chargedUpperUSD":0.8495499999999998}
{"turn":2,"elapsedMs":56203,"answer":"Chapter 14’s **ADHD evidence is mixed**. [S1]\n\n- **Positive trial:** An eight-week adjunctive omega-3 randomized trial (103 children) reported lower IL-6 and improved parent-rated symptoms. Parallel changes do not prove that IL-6 reduction caused improvement. [S2]\n- **Null/non-specific trials:** Vitamin D did not significantly reduce IL-6 versus placebo after three months. [S3] In a probiotic pilot, IL-6 fell in both groups—not evidence of a probiotic-specific effect—despite improved child-reported quality of life. [S4]\n- **Pooled evidence:** A 2021 meta-analysis found no significant IL-6 difference (p=0.09), with few cytokine studies. [S5] A 2022 analysis found higher IL-6, but significance disappeared when individual studies were removed. [S6] A 2026 network meta-analysis highlighted elevated IL-6 in autism; that finding cannot be transferred to ADHD. [S7]\n\nLimited, heterogeneous evidence and abstract-based omega-3/pooled appraisals constrain certainty. Neither IL-6 as a routine ADHD treatment target nor an OXTR mechanism is established. [S1]","chargedUpperUSD":1.5090124999999999}
